# Summarization

**Summarization** is the condensing of a large source into a shorter text that
keeps what matters for a given purpose. The summary is about the same thing as
the source and says less of it. It is the operation a language model is most
readily asked to perform, and for that reason it is the one most often
mistaken for the others: for [[abstraction]], for [[generalization]], and for
compression in the technical sense.

## Description

### What it is

In the discussion's table of operations, summarization takes a large source
and returns condensed information. Two things define it.

- **It is lossy on purpose.** Something is left out, and what is left out is
  chosen.
- **It is relative to a purpose.** The same paper has one summary for a
  referee and another for a newcomer. There is no summary without a reader in
  mind, which is why the definition says "what matters for its purpose".

### What it is not

The thread on this operation began with an objection: abstract thinking and
generating an abstract summary of a paper are not the same thing. The
distinctions that followed are the content of this card.

| Compared with | Summarization | The other operation |
|---|---|---|
| [[abstraction]] | shortens a text about the same subject | identifies a structure that applies beyond the source |
| [[generalization]] | covers the same cases in fewer words | covers more cases |
| [[simplification]] | stays true to the source, omitting | replaces the source with an easier, approximate model |
| compression | discards content judged unimportant | shortens the encoding and loses nothing |

The last row needs care, because the discussion also treats compression at
length. In [[compression-and-prediction]], compression means a shorter code
from which the data can be recovered exactly, and its length is tied to how
well a model predicts. A summary cannot be expanded back into its source. The
two share the word "shorter" and little else. A good summary may still rest on
a good model of the source, since deciding what matters requires
understanding it, but that is a claim about how summaries get made and not a
definition.

The word "abstract" is the other trap. The abstract at the head of a paper is
a summary. It need not contain a single abstraction.

### As a task for a language model

The question that brought summarization into the discussion named it, with
generalization, as one of the tasks language models are given. It is a task
they perform fluently, and fluency is the difficulty: a summary reads well
whether or not it is faithful. Among the operations in the table it has the
weakest built-in check. A deduction can be verified step by step and a
prediction can be tested, while a summary can only be compared with its source
against a stated purpose. A system that offers summarization as an operation
should therefore carry the purpose as an input and the source as provenance,
so that the comparison remains possible.

### In this vault

Every card here has a section of neighbouring concepts in which linked cards
are restated in two sentences from the point of view of the card at hand. Those
are summaries written for a purpose, the purpose being the reader of that one
card. The future lens [[neighbour-summary]] proposes to have an agent generate
them. The distinction above then becomes practical: such a lens produces
summaries, and it should not be expected to discover the relations between
cards, which is a matter of abstraction and [[analogy]].

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: large source in, condensed information out.
- It is not [[abstraction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a shorter text, against a structure.
- It is not [[generalization]] {relation: distinguished-from}: the same cases in fewer words, against more cases.
- It is not the compression of [[compression-and-prediction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a summary cannot be decoded back into its source.
- It is not [[simplification]] {relation: distinguished-from}: omission, against approximation.
- It is not [[analogy]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which relates two sources and shortens neither.
- Its faithfulness is a matter for [[evaluation]] {relation: uses} against a stated purpose.
- It is the operation the lens [[neighbour-summary]] {relation: uses} would automate.
- It bears on the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}: it is the first operation one would ask an agent cell to perform, and the hardest to check.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[abstraction]].** Identifying structure apart from incidental detail. It
  is the operation that the word "abstract" wrongly suggests a summary
  performs.
- **[[generalization]].** Widening a concept or claim to more cases. It was
  named together with summarization as a model's task, and goes the opposite
  way: outward where a summary goes inward.
- **[[compression-and-prediction]].** The link between code length and
  predictive performance. It concerns exact encoding, where summarization
  concerns chosen omission.
- **[[evaluation]].** Assessing a claim against a criterion. For a summary the
  criterion is its purpose and the evidence is its source.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[summarization]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[summarization]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[neighbour-summary]] is this
operation written as an agent query over the links of a card.

